{
	auto_https off
	admin off
}

# Keep the browser satellite on the same HTTPS origin as the dashboard. The
# bridge still performs its own TLS internally, but that certificate never
# reaches household browsers; Caddy terminates the public connection and
# forwards the WebSocket over loopback.
#
# On iridium this is a genuine loopback hop: the voice stack is now co-resident
# with the dashboard rather than a LAN round trip away.
(nova_routes) {
	# Token-gated route onto nova-voice-llm's llama-server (127.0.0.1:8765,
	# loopback-only by design). Added 2026-08-28 for unattended scripts
	# (budget's email-bill scraper, mail-archive's mailbox categoriser) that
	# need LLM classification but have no live Claude session to do it in.
	#
	# The token is {$NOVA_LLM_KEY} — a Caddyfile-level env substitution, NOT a
	# literal, because this repo has a public GitHub remote (origin) and a
	# literal secret here would leak on the next push. nova-caddy.service
	# loads it from EnvironmentFile=-/etc/nova-caddy.env, which is NOT part of
	# this repo and is not touched by deploy-nova-dashboard.ps1 — create it
	# once by hand on each host that needs this route:
	#   echo 'NOVA_LLM_KEY=<token>' | sudo tee /etc/nova-caddy.env
	# The actual token value is recorded in mail-archive/credentials.md
	# (that repo has no remote at all).
	#
	# Checked in HERE, not just applied live on iridium: deploy-nova-dashboard.ps1
	# installs this file over /etc/caddy/nova.Caddyfile on every dashboard
	# deploy, so a hand-edit on the live host does not survive the next deploy.
	# Confirmed the hard way: a live-only edit was silently wiped mid-run by a
	# dashboard deploy, and ~40k mailbox-categorisation LLM calls failed against
	# the dashboard's own 404 page for the rest of that run before anyone
	# noticed, because @unauthed's "not header" fallback only 403s a REQUEST
	# that reaches this Caddyfile at all — once the route itself was gone, every
	# call fell through to the plain dashboard proxy below and got a 200-with-
	# wrong-body/404, not a loud failure.
	# The matcher shape below was rewritten 2026-09-02 during the face-auth
	# review. The PRE-EXISTING form was:
	#     @authed header X-Nova-LLM-Key {$NOVA_LLM_KEY}
	#     @unauthed not header X-Nova-LLM-Key {$NOVA_LLM_KEY}
	# with the substitution UNQUOTED, which is a genuine defect worth writing
	# down because it is invisible until the day it bites. {$VAR} is textual
	# substitution performed BEFORE the Caddyfile is parsed, so an unset
	# NOVA_LLM_KEY leaves the literal token list `header X-Nova-LLM-Key` —
	# a one-argument header matcher. Measured against the Caddy actually
	# installed here (v2.11.2, `caddy adapt` from stdin, 2026-09-02):
	#   header X-K          -> "Error: malformed header matcher: expected both
	#                           field and value" — a hard PARSE failure, so
	#                           `caddy validate` does NOT pass and Caddy would
	#                           refuse to start at all. Not fail-open on this
	#                           version, but a total-ingress-outage risk.
	#   header X-K ""       -> {"X-K":[""]} — an empty-string EQUALITY match.
	#                           Fails closed for every real value, but still
	#                           matches a deliberately empty header, which any
	#                           caller can send (`curl -H "X-Nova-LLM-Key;"`).
	# So quoting alone is necessary but not sufficient. The `route` block below
	# is what makes it airtight: `route` evaluates in FILE order (ordinary
	# directives inside `handle` are re-sorted into Caddy's standard directive
	# order, where reverse_proxy runs before respond — so a plain `respond 403`
	# guard placed above would never fire). Explicit empty-value denial first,
	# then the equality check, then a default deny. Every path out of this block
	# is a decision, none is a fall-through.
	#
	# The value still resolves from EnvironmentFile=-/etc/nova-caddy.env; see
	# deploy-nova-dashboard.ps1, which now checks that file for a non-empty
	# NOVA_LLM_KEY/NOVA_FACE_KEY before running `caddy validate`.
	handle_path /internal-llm/* {
		route {
			@empty_llm_key header X-Nova-LLM-Key ""
			respond @empty_llm_key 403
			@authed_llm header X-Nova-LLM-Key "{$NOVA_LLM_KEY}"
			reverse_proxy @authed_llm 127.0.0.1:8765
			respond 403
		}
	}

	# Token-gated route onto nova-face-auth (127.0.0.1:8099, the face
	# recognition/liveness/passkey service). Exists because browser callers need
	# a secure context at all: MediaRecorder/getUserMedia are unavailable over
	# plain HTTP, so the kiosk and browser satellites cannot reach
	# http://iridium:8099 directly — this route is not a convenience.
	#
	# {$NOVA_FACE_KEY} is an env substitution, NOT a literal, for the same reason
	# as NOVA_LLM_KEY above: this repo has a public GitHub remote and a literal
	# secret here leaks on the next push. It resolves from
	# EnvironmentFile=-/etc/nova-caddy.env, created by hand once per host and NOT
	# part of this repo or touched by deploy-nova-dashboard.ps1:
	#   echo 'NOVA_FACE_KEY=<token>' | sudo tee -a /etc/nova-caddy.env
	# The same value goes in /etc/nova-face-auth.env, which is what the service
	# itself checks — Caddy's header match here is only a perimeter convenience,
	# the service enforces X-Nova-Face-Key on every endpoint including /healthz,
	# so bypassing Caddy on the LAN port gains nothing.
	#
	# This route is for NON-BROWSER LAN callers (scripts, satellites, other
	# hosts) that can hold the shared key. Browsers must NOT use it: they go
	# through the dashboard's own same-origin /api/face/* proxy, which attaches
	# the key server-side, so the key never reaches a browser at all.
	# Same three-step `route` shape as /internal-llm/* above, for the reasons
	# measured and written up there: quote the substitution so an unset
	# NOVA_FACE_KEY cannot refuse the whole config and take every Caddy route
	# down with it, and deny the empty-value header explicitly first, because a
	# quoted-but-unset key degrades to an empty-string equality match that any
	# caller can satisfy with `curl -H "X-Nova-Face-Key;"`.
	handle_path /face/* {
		route {
			@empty_face_key header X-Nova-Face-Key ""
			respond @empty_face_key 403
			@authed_face header X-Nova-Face-Key "{$NOVA_FACE_KEY}"
			reverse_proxy @authed_face 127.0.0.1:8099
			respond 403
		}
	}

	@voice path /voice-satellite
	handle @voice {
		reverse_proxy https://127.0.0.1:8767 {
			transport http {
				# The upstream is loopback-only from Caddy's perspective and uses
				# the household certificate, whose names intentionally do not
				# include 127.0.0.1.
				tls_insecure_skip_verify
			}
		}
	}

	handle {
		# authentik/specs/authentik-sso.md task 4: Next.js moved off :80 to
		# 127.0.0.1:3001 (NOVA_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1, PORT=3001 in
		# ops/iridium/nova-ha-dashboard.service) so Caddy is the ONLY ingress —
		# a prerequisite for task 5's /config auth gate to mean anything at
		# all. Checked in here for the same reason as the rest of this file:
		# a live-only edit does not survive the next dashboard deploy.
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
	}
}

# authentik/specs/authentik-sso.md task 5: gate the dashboard's admin/config
# surface behind authentik forward-auth (domain-level proxy provider
# "nova-dashboard-config", bound to the embedded outpost). Caddy evaluates
# `handle` blocks in file order within a site (unlike most directives), so
# these two matchers are `handle`d ahead of nova_routes' own catch-all
# `handle {}` — that ordering is what makes the gate take effect at all.
#
# Two path-shape corrections made against the drafted matcher list in the
# plan, both found by reading the actual route handlers rather than trusting
# the draft:
#   - /api/config/client is a read-only, sanitized subset fetched by
#     MapPanel, ClimateControls, radar, useBedroomHeaterConfig and
#     TasksPanel — all live on the KIOSK dashboard, not just /config. It is
#     explicitly excluded from @admin_any (which has no method filter and
#     would otherwise gate every method on it, including the kiosk's GETs).
#     Everything else under /api/config/** is admin-only (ConfigWorkspace).
#   - /api/update itself is a GET-only status read consumed by UpdateBanner,
#     which renders unconditionally on the main Dashboard component — also
#     kiosk-facing. Only the mutating /api/update/* actions (apply, rollback,
#     check, settings) are gated, via @admin_writes' method filter, not the
#     unconditional @admin_any.
#   - /api/modules is deliberately NOT blanket-gated on path alone: the
#     module runtime (app/components/modules/ModuleHost.tsx) fires
#     `POST /api/modules/events` from every loaded module instance
#     (including on the kiosk) to deliver events, and routes arbitrary
#     methods to a module's own `[id]/[...path]` handler for the module's own
#     UI actions (e.g. the Discord module's dry-run confirm). Neither is an
#     admin action. Only the true admin surface is gated: `POST /api/modules`
#     (install), `PATCH`/`DELETE /api/modules/{id}` (enable/disable/reload/
#     uninstall — exactly one path segment, so it cannot collide with the
#     module-owned `[...path]` catch-all which needs at least one segment
#     past the id), and `PUT /api/modules/{id}/config`. `/api/modules/events`
#     is explicitly excluded since it has the same one-segment shape as
#     `/api/modules/{id}`.
(config_gate_authed) {
	@admin_any {
		path /config /config/* /api/config /api/config/* /api/preferences/history /api/system/* /api/voice/administration /api/voice/training/* /api/face/arm /api/face/health /api/kiosk/witness/activity
		not path /api/config/client
	}
	# Face routes and this gate, decided 2026-09-03.
	#
	# Adeline: "I don't care about spoofing. this is a convenience thing. assume
	# that nobody with malicious intent will have access to my house."
	#
	# So /api/face/enrol* and /api/face/subjects* are NOT gated here any more.
	# They sit behind the /config page gate already — the enrolment UI cannot be
	# reached without passing it — and gating the XHR a second time bought
	# nothing she values while adding a real failure: the outpost answers an
	# unauthenticated fetch with a 302 to authentik, and a cross-origin redirect
	# on an XHR surfaces in the browser as an opaque CORS error rather than
	# "log in again". That is what broke live enrolment.
	#
	# /api/face/arm and /api/face/health STAY gated. `arm` clears a lockout, so
	# it is the one face route that grants rather than merely records, and
	# `health` reports the enrolled headcount. Both are page loads or admin
	# actions, not XHR in a capture loop, so the redirect behaviour is correct
	# for them.
	#
	# Whatever is gated must be matched by PATH in @admin_any, never by method in
	# @admin_writes: that matcher carries `method PUT POST PATCH DELETE`, so a GET
	# matches neither and falls through completely ungated. During this build that
	# left GET on the subject thumbnail — the biometric itself — readable by
	# anyone on the LAN.
	@admin_writes {
		method PUT POST PATCH DELETE
		path /api/theme /api/design /api/layout /api/watchface /api/background-texture /api/theme-library /api/voice-personality-library /api/voice/engine /api/voice/options /api/voice/speaker-profiles /api/voice/speaker-profiles/* /api/voice/memories /api/camera/*/settings /api/camera/*/analysis* /api/update/* /api/modules /api/modules/* /api/modules/*/config
		# Caddy's `*` spans slashes, so /api/modules/* was matching the Discord
		# module's own attention route — the DM an agent sends before it stops to
		# ask a question — and answering the flat LAN 403. That route carries its
		# own x-nova-attention-key secret; it is authenticated, just not here.
		not path /api/modules/events /api/modules/*/attention
	}
	handle @admin_any {
		import ak_forward_auth
	}
	handle @admin_writes {
		import ak_forward_auth
	}
}

# The outpost's own callback/session endpoints, served on the APPLICATION's
# origin rather than authentik's. Load-bearing, and not obvious: in
# `forward_domain` mode the outpost builds its OAuth `redirect_uri` as
# `<external_host>/outpost.goauthentik.io/callback` — i.e. on :443 here, NOT on
# authentik's own :9443. Without this snippet that path falls through to
# nova_routes' catch-all `handle { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001 }` and Next.js
# answers its own 404, so the browser completes the authorize step, comes back
# with a valid `code`, and the outpost never sees it — no session cookie is ever
# set and /config redirects forever. Found live: the 404 carried
# `x-nextjs-prerender`, proving Next.js served it, while the same path on :9443
# returned a proper outpost 302.
#
# Must be imported AHEAD of nova_routes (Caddy evaluates `handle` blocks in file
# order within a site), for the same reason config_gate_authed must be.
#
# Proxied straight to the loopback authentik server rather than back out through
# :9443 — one less TLS hop, and it cannot break if the :9443 vhost changes.
# `header_up Host` pins the provider lookup, which reads the RAW Host header
# (never X-Forwarded-Host) and must equal this provider's external_host exactly,
# no port — the same rule ak_forward_auth documents below.
#
# LAN vhosts deliberately do NOT import this: they use config_gate_denied (a
# flat 403, no login path at all), so no callback can ever be issued for them
# and exposing authentik endpoints there would widen the surface for nothing.
(ak_outpost_routes) {
	handle /outpost.goauthentik.io/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9000 {
			header_up Host nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net
		}
	}
}

# authentik's flow executor, served on the DASHBOARD's own origin so the Nova
# login surface can drive it. `specs/login-surface.md` owns the design.
#
# Two independent constraints force this, and either one alone would be enough:
#
#   - authentik sends no CORS headers at all, so a fetch from the dashboard page
#     to :9443 is cross-origin (the port differs) and is blocked outright.
#   - authentik derives the WebAuthn RP ID and the expected origin FROM THE
#     REQUEST (stages/authenticator_webauthn/utils.py, get_rp_id/get_origin) and
#     offers no setting for either. A flow served on :9443 expects an assertion
#     whose origin is https://nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net:9443; a browser on a :443
#     page produces the port-less form. Without this snippet the passkey and
#     face buttons cannot work at all.
#
# `header_up Host` is the same rule ak_outpost_routes and ak_forward_auth both
# document: authentik reads the RAW Host header, never X-Forwarded-Host. Three
# snippets now depend on it.
#
# Scope is the executor API and the flow-cancel endpoint. /static/** and
# /if/flow/** are deliberately NOT proxied: the dashboard renders its own login
# UI and loads none of authentik's assets. Proxy the minimum of another
# application onto your own origin.
#
# Never gated, and cannot be: this IS the login. What it exposes is already
# anonymous by design — a GET of the passkey-login executor hands a WebAuthn
# challenge to an unauthenticated caller today, on :9443, because that is what a
# login endpoint does.
#
# NOT imported by the LAN vhosts. They use config_gate_denied, a flat 403 with
# no login path, so a login endpoint there would be a route to nowhere.
#
# `header_down Location` repairs the prefix on the way back out, and it is not
# cosmetic. `handle_path` STRIPS `/authentik` before authentik sees the request,
# so every redirect authentik writes is built from the stripped path — a stage
# POST that SUCCEEDS answers
#     302 Location: /api/v3/flows/executor/<slug>/?query=
# On this origin that path is Next.js, which answers 308 and then a 404 HTML
# page. On 2026-09-04 that is exactly where every sign-in died: authentik logged
# "Successful authentication", the browser followed the redirect off the proxy
# into a 404, and the surface reported a transport failure. Rewriting the
# Location keeps the redirect inside the proxy. Absolute URLs are left alone —
# `^/` cannot match them.
#
# The login surface no longer depends on this (it takes the redirect manually;
# see `lib/authentik-flow.ts`), but anything else pointed at `/authentik/*`
# would fall into the same hole without it.
(ak_flow_routes) {
	handle_path /authentik/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9000 {
			header_up Host nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net
			header_down Location "^/" "/authentik/"
		}
	}
}

# "Is anyone signed in?", answered with a STATUS CODE rather than a redirect.
#
# This is the one gated route in the estate that deliberately breaks the
# redirect pattern, and the reason is a live failure: on 2026-09-03 face
# enrolment broke because a gated XHR received the outpost's 302 to authentik,
# and a cross-origin redirect on a fetch surfaces in the browser as an opaque
# CORS error. "Opaque CORS error" is not something a UI can render as "sign in".
#
# So handle_response turns the 302 into a bare 401 and the client can read it.
# Any future route whose job is reporting auth state to a fetch uses this shape.
#
# Must be imported AHEAD of config_gate_authed and nova_routes: `handle` blocks
# evaluate in file order within a site, and the catch-all would otherwise take
# this path first.
(ak_auth_probe) {
	handle /api/auth/whoami {
		forward_auth https://nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net:9443 {
			uri /outpost.goauthentik.io/auth/traefik
			header_up Host nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net
			copy_headers X-authentik-username X-authentik-groups X-authentik-email
			@unauthenticated status 302
			handle_response @unauthenticated {
				respond 401
			}
		}
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
	}
}

(ak_forward_auth) {
	# forward_auth only gatekeeps: on a 2xx from the outpost it falls through
	# to the NEXT directive rather than to the app, so the reverse_proxy below
	# is required here — without it, an authenticated request that matched
	# @admin_any/@admin_writes would fall through this `handle` block with no
	# response at all, since `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and
	# nova_routes' own `handle { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001 }` never runs
	# for a request that already matched this one.
	forward_auth https://nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net:9443 {
		# NOT /auth/caddy -- that endpoint does not exist in this authentik
		# version (goauthentik.io/outpost 2026.8.0) and 404s unconditionally,
		# regardless of headers. Verified live: /auth/traefik correctly
		# returns a 302 to the authorize endpoint for an unauthenticated
		# request whose Host matches the provider's external_host, while
		# /auth/caddy 404s under the exact same conditions. authentik ships
		# generic reverse-proxy forward-auth handlers named after
		# traefik/nginx, not one per proxy product; Caddy uses the traefik
		# one, matching upstream authentik documentation for Caddy setups.
		uri /outpost.goauthentik.io/auth/traefik
		# Caddy's forward_auth otherwise sends Host: nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net:9443
		# (the auth server's own dial address) to the auth-check request.
		# authentik's domain-level provider lookup matches against the raw
		# Host header, not X-Forwarded-Host, so a :9443-suffixed Host 404s
		# even though X-Forwarded-Host is already correct -- verified live by
		# comparing a manual request with an overridden bare Host header
		# (302, correct redirect to the authorize endpoint) against the same
		# request with the default Host (404). This must match the
		# provider's external_host exactly: no port.
		header_up Host nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net
		copy_headers X-authentik-username X-authentik-groups X-authentik-email
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
}

(config_gate_denied) {
	@admin_any {
		path /config /config/* /api/config /api/config/* /api/preferences/history /api/system/* /api/voice/administration /api/voice/training/* /api/face/arm /api/face/health /api/kiosk/witness/activity
		not path /api/config/client
	}
	# Same face-route reasoning as config_gate_authed above. Note the consequence
	# on the LAN vhosts specifically: this snippet answers a flat 403 with no
	# login path, so face ENROLMENT is not possible over the LAN identity at all
	# — it must be done from the tailnet origin, which is also the only origin
	# whose certificate is publicly trusted and therefore the only one where
	# getUserMedia will hand the capture UI a camera. That is deliberate.
	@admin_writes {
		method PUT POST PATCH DELETE
		path /api/theme /api/design /api/layout /api/watchface /api/background-texture /api/theme-library /api/voice-personality-library /api/voice/engine /api/voice/options /api/voice/speaker-profiles /api/voice/speaker-profiles/* /api/voice/memories /api/camera/*/settings /api/camera/*/analysis* /api/update/* /api/modules /api/modules/* /api/modules/*/config
		# Caddy's `*` spans slashes, so /api/modules/* was matching the Discord
		# module's own attention route — the DM an agent sends before it stops to
		# ask a question — and answering the flat LAN 403. That route carries its
		# own x-nova-attention-key secret; it is authenticated, just not here.
		not path /api/modules/events /api/modules/*/attention
	}
	handle @admin_any {
		respond 403
	}
	handle @admin_writes {
		respond 403
	}
}

# Canonical household URL over the tailnet. Caddy asks the local Tailscale
# daemon for this publicly trusted certificate and renews it automatically.
#
# THIS MUST BE THE NAME THIS NODE ACTUALLY OWNS. tailscaled will only issue a
# certificate for its own MagicDNS name; point this at a name it does not own and
# Caddy fails to start, taking HTTPS (and the browser voice satellite) down with
# it.
#
# Do not hand-edit this to follow a host move. `ops/iridium/sync-tailnet-host.sh`
# rewrites it to whatever name tailscaled reports, and both bootstrap-iridium.sh
# and deploy-nova-dashboard.ps1 run it before `caddy validate`, so the value here
# is only ever a starting point.
#

# Plain HTTP on the tailnet name. Without this block the Host matches no site
# at all and Caddy answers 200 with a zero-length body (auto_https is off, so
# there is no implicit redirect). That is indistinguishable from "the page
# loaded" to curl and to the kiosk CDP probe, and renders as a white screen —
# it cost a kiosk cutover on 2026-09-03 before anyone read Content-Length.
# Redirect instead: the tailnet HTTPS origin is the only one the config gate
# and face auth accept, so every plain-HTTP hit belongs there.
http://nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net {
	redir https://{host}{uri} 308
}

# History worth keeping: the retired nova-vm node held `nova` and logging it out
# did NOT release the name — it sat "offline, last seen 1m ago" still holding it,
# and the CLI could not claim it either (`tailscale set --hostname` was a silent
# no-op; `tailscale cert` refused with `must be one of
# ["iridium.tuatara-dory.ts.net"]`). It took deleting the old machine and
# renaming this node in the admin console. Reclaimed 2026-08-03.
https://nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net:443 {
	tls {
		get_certificate tailscale
	}
	# Order matters: the outpost's own callback endpoints, the proxied flow
	# executor and the auth probe must all be claimed before nova_routes'
	# catch-all can swallow them into Next.js.
	import ak_outpost_routes
	import ak_flow_routes
	import ak_auth_probe
	import config_gate_authed
	import nova_routes
}

# LAN fallbacks remain available for already-enrolled household-CA devices.
# They are not the documented browser-voice URL because new devices would need
# the private CA installed before getUserMedia is available.
#
# nova-vm's addresses (192.168.8.14, 100.66.54.105, nova-vm[.local]) are gone:
# the hosting role collapsed onto iridium's own .20 identity.
https://nova.local:443, https://nova:443, https://iridium.local:443, https://iridium:443, https://192.168.8.20:443, https://100.104.57.108:443 {
	tls /opt/nova-ha-dashboard/data/nova-dashboard-tls/server.crt /opt/nova-ha-dashboard/data/nova-dashboard-tls/server.key
	import config_gate_denied
	import nova_routes
}

# Plain-HTTP LAN identities. This is the kiosk's actual access path
# (NOVA_BACKEND_URL=http://127.0.0.1/ on nocturnium resolves through the LAN
# to this host's :80, and the Apple TV app's default is http://nova.local) —
# it used to reach Next.js directly because nothing was listening here at
# all; now that Next is loopback-only (task 4), this block is what keeps that
# path alive, just routed through Caddy instead. `auto_https off` is set at
# the top of this file, so a bare :80 block is NOT auto-upgraded into an
# HTTPS redirect (verified against this Caddy version/config before relying
# on it) — without that, this block would redirect the kiosk to :443 and it
# has no way to accept that redirect meaningfully for a plain HTTP request
# from a device with no household CA installed.
http://nova.local, http://nova, http://iridium.local, http://iridium, http://192.168.8.20, http://100.104.57.108 {
	# /face/* is HTTPS-only. nova_routes below defines it, and this block would
	# otherwise serve it in the clear: every non-browser caller authenticates
	# with the X-Nova-Face-Key shared header, and Nova is WiFi-only, so a
	# plaintext :80 face route puts that key on the air on every call. Claimed
	# here, ahead of `import nova_routes`, because `handle` blocks are evaluated
	# in file order within a site.
	#
	# 421 rather than 403: the request is not forbidden, it arrived on the wrong
	# origin, and the message says so. Nothing legitimate uses this path over
	# :80 — browsers cannot (getUserMedia needs a secure context) and the LAN
	# HTTPS vhost above is available to anything holding the household CA.
	handle /face/* {
		respond "face auth requires HTTPS; use the tailnet origin" 421
	}
	# /api/face/* is blocked here for the same reason, extended to cover what the
	# first version of this guard missed. The shared key never crosses the wire
	# on these routes (the dashboard proxy injects it server-side), but the
	# REQUEST BODY of /api/face/assert and /api/face/enrol is a one-second video
	# of somebody's face, and the RESPONSE carries the WebAuthn assertion. On a
	# WiFi-only network that is worse than the key.
	#
	# Nothing legitimate is lost: browsers cannot reach these over :80 anyway,
	# because getUserMedia and MediaRecorder are unavailable outside a secure
	# context, so there is no client that can produce a clip to post here.
	handle /api/face/* {
		respond "face auth requires HTTPS; use the tailnet origin" 421
	}
	import config_gate_denied
	import nova_routes
}

# authentik SSO (authentik/specs/authentik-sso.md, task 2). authentik's own
# server container is published loopback-only (127.0.0.1:9000) — this is the
# only way anything reaches it. A dedicated port (:9443), not a path under the
# :443 vhost above, so a broken or being-upgraded authentik can never take the
# kiosk/Apple TV dashboard down with it — those two vhosts and this one don't
# share a handle block, don't share nova_routes, and Caddy reloading this file
# for an authentik change cannot touch dashboard routing above. Same
# `tls { get_certificate tailscale }` pattern as the :443 vhost: this node's
# own MagicDNS name is nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net (see the history note above),
# so this must stay "nova.…", never "iridium.…".
#
# Address is bare ":9443", NOT "https://nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net:9443" — a
# hostname in the site address makes Caddy match incoming requests on the
# HTTP Host header too, not just bind the listener/TLS cert. That broke every
# forward_auth caller whose own external_host differs from nova's own name
# (found live, budget/specs/system-overview.md task 6): a forward_auth call
# from Indium's Caddy necessarily sets `Host: indium.tuatara-dory.ts.net:7001`
# so that authentik's own per-provider lookup (which reads the raw Host
# header, confirmed by direct testing — an explicit X-Forwarded-Host does NOT
# override it) resolves to the *budget-site* provider instead of whichever
# provider happens to share nova's own hostname. With a hostname'd site
# address, that Host mismatch made this vhost match no route at all and
# silently return a blank 200 — auth FAILED OPEN, not closed. `tls
# nova.tuatara-dory.ts.net { get_certificate tailscale }` keeps the cert
# selection exactly as before; only the HTTP-level host gate is removed. This
# port is still loopback-proxied to authentik only, still fully separate from
# the dashboard vhosts above, so the isolation the original comment describes
# is unchanged.
:9443 {
	tls {
		get_certificate tailscale
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:9000
}
